WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.901
Limitations and risks: where the evidence stops.

2
00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:05.784
A limitations section is not an apology.

3
00:00:06.064 --> 00:00:09.371
It tells readers where your evidence ends, so they don’t stretch it.

4
00:00:09.651 --> 00:00:28.125
Model cards, proposed by Mitchell and colleagues in 2019 as a standard for reporting on models, ask authors to state intended uses, uses that are out of scope, and caveats, precisely so that someone deciding whether to adopt a model can check it against their own situation.

5
00:00:28.405 --> 00:00:30.411
The same structure works for a system.

6
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:34.808
Include specific failure examples with how often each kind occurs.

7
00:00:35.088 --> 00:00:42.981
Name the users, inputs, and conditions you didn’t test (“English only; RFPs under 80 pages; no scanned documents”).

8
00:00:43.261 --> 00:00:49.704
Publish the final risk register, including risks that never materialized, because it shows what you looked for.

9
00:00:49.984 --> 00:01:02.080
State the small-sample caveats honestly: “no successful injections in 24 attempts” is consistent with a real success rate of a few percent or more, as the Wilson exercise showed.

10
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.651
And say what you would advise against: “Not suitable as the only check before a bid submission.

11
00:01:07.931 --> 00:01:08.228
”.

12
00:01:08.508 --> 00:01:11.346
Reviewers read this section to judge your judgment.

13
00:01:11.626 --> 00:01:15.508
A short or vague one suggests you either didn’t look or didn’t want to say.
